{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4Uw7MUNhCtTCTGyTPCA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found no P0, and none of the declared conditions is false against this payload's code. There is one undeclared P1 (below, for the annex) and eight P2 follow-ups. The decision field carries my recommendation; the P1 should go into the signed material as an open item.\n\n**What I checked and found correct**\n- **Load order:** `check_bash_safety.py` runs the eight fragments in its own namespace in a fixed order. They join end to end without a gap, and every module-level dependency is defined before it is used (`_e3_basename` in `fact_canonical.py:311` comes before `posture_tables.py`).\n- **Moved code (item 4):** the old single-file hook already used `from __future__ import annotations` (checked against `.claude/plans/PLAN-182/staged-w1/tree/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py:60`), so annotations behave the same. `__file__`, the `__main__` guard and patching through the facade also behave as before.\n- **Install and protection:** the installer copies `_lib` subfolders whole (`scripts/install.sh:1520-1547`). `.claude/hooks/_lib/**/*.py` in `canonical_edit/guards.py:34` protects every new fragment, and `check_arbitration_kernel.py:314` lists `_bash_safety_parts`.\n- **Condition 6 is true:** both switches are read from the startup snapshot of `CEO_*` variables (`trusted_env.py:36`). Only an exact `0` turns them off, and if the snapshot cannot be read the guard stays on.\n- **Fail direction (item 2):** a missing or broken fragment or module makes the hook crash with no decision, so the command is allowed. That is the declared fail-open direction for infrastructure faults.\n- **Item 6:** standard library only, and no Python 3.10+ syntax in the three fragment folders.\n- **Conditions 5 and 7** are consistent with this payload.\n\n**P1 for the annex (undeclared): the new indirect-execution guard crashes and allows the command on deeply nested `xargs`**\n- **Where:**\n  - `.claude/hooks/_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py:386-393` recurses once per `xargs` (and at `:394-399` once per `find -exec`).\n  - `_scan_segment` (`:347`) never checks depth; `MAX_DEPTH` is only checked in `_scan` (`:472`).\n  - The call site `.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_scan.py:571-584` does not catch errors from the scan.\n  - `decisions_cli.py:243-249` turns any error into an allow.\n- **Failure:** a command like `xargs xargs … (about 1000 times) rm -rf ~` fits under the 1024-word and 16 KiB limits.\n  - The older checks never see `rm` (the first word is `xargs`).\n  - The new scan then hits Python's recursion limit and raises `RecursionError`, and `main()` allows the command with a \"FATAL\" line on stderr.\n  - GNU `xargs` then runs `rm -rf ~`.\n- **Why it matters:**\n  - This is fail-open on input the guard cannot handle, which item 2 forbids.\n  - The repo's own rule says the same (`posture_parser.py:369`: a `RecursionError` in a security matcher is a fail-open).\n  - It contradicts `CHANGELOG.md:93-94` (\"fails closed … at its depth, size and word limits\").\n  - It is not a P0: the base release never blocked `xargs rm -rf` at all, so nothing regresses, and the attack needs a deliberately built command of about 1000 words.\n- **Minimal fix:**\n  - Start `_scan_segment` with `if depth > MAX_DEPTH: return _hit(\"bash_indirect_limit\")`.\n  - Optionally, at `posture_scan.py:578`, treat a `RecursionError` from `scan()` as a `bash_indirect_limit` block, while a failed import stays fail-open.\n  - Add a regression test with about 1000 nested `xargs`.\n\n**P2 follow-ups**\n1. **Wrong pointer in the facade docstring.** `check_bash_safety.py:12` says `_apply_destructive_citation_gate` is in `rewrite_citation.py`; it is in `fact_state.py:4`. Someone re-applying a hand patch (condition 7) is sent to the wrong file. Fix the docstring.\n2. **Stale comment on what counts as destructive.** The header at `rewrite_citation.py:274` still lists only `rm -rf`, `git reset --hard` and forced `git push`. But `posture_scan.py:582` also marks every `bash_indirect_*` block as destructive, including the limit, unparseable and opaque-feeder ones. Those blocks can therefore be released by the opt-in fact gate or citation pilot. Update the comment, or exclude the limit and unparseable codes from release.\n3. **Long direct commands are blocked with a misleading message.** `bash_indirect_guard.py:472` blocks any command over 16384 characters or 1024 words, for example a large heredoc written through Bash or a `git add` with more than 1000 paths.\n   - The message tells the user to \"write the executable command in direct form\", which does not fit a command that is already direct.\n   - The adopter notes (`CHANGELOG.md:465-470`) do not mention the limits.\n   - This falls under condition 6's declared caveat about unmeasured false positives.\n   - Fix: give the limit block its own message and document the numbers.\n4. **Block telemetry is lost in the opt-in modes.** When the fact gate is in enforce mode, or the citation pilot reshapes an indirect block, the new decision drops `reason_code` (`fact_canonical.py:146-155`, `fact_state.py:23-65`). The `emit_veto_triggered` at `decisions_cli.py:195` is then skipped. Carry `reason_code` through.\n5. **Duplicated definitions left by the split:**\n   - `ceo-boot.py:120` defines `check_tier_a_spec_version_drift` again, but the check list at `scheduled_cache.py:376` already points to the copy in `model_persona.py:6`. The facade copy is never used by the list, so a hand patch to it does nothing.\n   - `budget-summary.py:400` repeats `_load_normalize_model_name` from `cost_rollup.py`.\n   - `native_reconciliation.py:3-6` repeats the `MEMORY_CLAIM_*` constants that set the defaults at `:566-569`. Editing the facade constants (`budget-summary.py:102-105`) will not change those defaults.\n   - Fix: keep one definition of each.\n6. **The facade's `__doc__` is replaced.** Each fragment's opening docstring is written into the facade, so `check_bash_safety.__doc__` ends up as the text from `decisions_cli.py`, and `ceo-boot.py` is affected the same way. I found nothing that reads it; cosmetic.\n7. **Leftover old docstring.** `normalization.py:4-55` carries a second copy of the old module docstring, with a stray `#!` line in the middle and steps numbered 1, 2, 4, 5. Remove it.\n8. **Infrastructure faults and scanner bugs look the same.** The import at `posture_scan.py:571` is outside any `try`. If `bash_indirect_guard.py` is missing, every command that passes the older checks is allowed with a \"FATAL\" line. The direction is right, but in `main()` a missing file and a scanner bug cannot be told apart. Separate them as part of the P1 fix."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4132,"cache_read_input_tokens":217246,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4132},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd5ebd56-59b6-4268-812f-e9994cacc38a","uuid":"b911dd34-21da-4c1b-9b25-2dc1973bc62f","timestamp":"2026-11-15T09:32:53.245Z","request_id":"req_011Ch4Uw77an19mTb8WAoGdu"}
