-summary.py:371-397) rebinds every part function to the facade globals. Every global the parts use is defined in the facade. Part defaults reference only names local to the part. _lib is a regular package, so the __init__-less _budget_summary_parts imports as a namespace subpackage on Python 3.9.\n- Installer: scripts/install.sh:1520-1547 cp -R's _lib subdirectories, so the three fragment directories ship. The canonical guard `.claude/hooks/_lib/**/*.py` (canonical_edit/guards.py:34) covers them. check_arbitration_kernel.py:314 lists _bash_safety_parts (condition 5).\n- Condition 6 holds: posture_scan.py:572-580 reads CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD / CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS from the trusted_env snapshot (CEO_ prefix, trusted_env.py:36). Only \"0\" disables. A missing snapshot leaves the guard ON.\n- Fail direction: a missing or broken fragment or module, or an import error, makes the hook crash with no decision, and the shim lets the command through (fail-open on infra, doctrine). The fact gate stays fail-open on infra and closed on citation verification (moved code, unchanged).\n- Item 6: stdlib only; no 3.10+ syntax in the three fragment directories.\n- Conditions 5 and 7 are consistent with this payload; no declared condition is contradicted.\n\nP0: none.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\nP1 — .claude/hooks/_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py:386-393 (xargs; also :394-399 find -exec), with call site .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_scan.py:571-584 and catch-all .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/decisions_cli.py:243-249.\nFailure scenario: _scan_segment (bash_indirect_guard.py:347) recurses once per xargs level with no depth check; MAX_DEPTH is enforced only in _scan (:472). A command `xargs xargs ...(about 1000 times) rm -rf ~` stays under MAX_WORDS=1024 and MAX_BODY_BYTES=16384. The legacy matchers never see `rm` (tokens[0] is xargs; _e3 and _e4 walk iteratively without hitting it). The new scan then raises RecursionError at the default limit of 1000. decide_command does not catch it, and main()'s `except Exception` emits allow with a FATAL line on stderr, so GNU xargs runs rm -rf ~. This is fail-OPEN on input a security matcher cannot handle (item 2). The repo's own doctrine calls this out: posture_parser.py:369 says a RecursionError in a security matcher is a crash, i.e. a fail-open. It also contradicts CHANGELOG.md:93-94 (\"fails closed ... at its depth, size and word limits\"). It is not a regression vs the base, which never blocked xargs-wrapped rm -rf at all, and it needs a deliberately built ~1000-token command, so it is not P0.\nMinimal fix: add `if depth > MAX_DEPTH: return _hit(\"bash_indirect_limit\")` at the top of _scan_segment. Optionally, at posture_scan.py:578, map a RecursionError from scan() to a bash_indirect_limit block while keeping ImportError fail-open. Add a regression test with ~1000 nested xargs.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. check_bash_safety.py:12 (facade docstring) says _apply_destructive_citation_gate is implemented in rewrite_citation.py; it is at fact_state.py:4. A maintainer re-applying a hand patch (condition 7) is sent to the wrong file. Fix: correct the marker.\n2. rewrite_citation.py:274 still defines DESTRUCTIVE as rm -rf / git reset --hard / git push --force. posture_scan.py:582 now marks every bash_indirect_* hit destructive=True, including bash_indirect_limit, bash_indirect_unparseable_shell and bash_indirect_opaque_stdin, so they become releasable through the opt-in fact gate (enforce) or the citation pilot. Fix: update the comment, or exclude the limit/unparseable codes from release.\n3. bash_indirect_guard.py:472 blocks any command longer than 16384 characters or 1024 words, even a direct one (a large heredoc written through Bash, `git add` with more than 1000 paths). The block reason tells the user to \"write the executable command in direct form\", which misleads for a command that is already direct. CHANGELOG.md:465-470 (adopter notes) omits the caps; :93-94 mentions them only generically. This is within condition 6's declared unmeasured-false-positive caveat. Fix: a distinct reason text for the limit block, and document the numbers.\n4. When the fact gate in enforce mode (fact_canonical.py:146-155) or the citation pilot (fact_state.py:23-65) reshapes an indirect block, the new Decision drops reason_code, so emit_veto_triggered(reason_code=bash_indirect_*) at decisions_cli.py:195 is skipped in those opt-in modes. Fix: carry reason_code through.\n5. Duplicates left by the split, a drift trap that condition 7 itself warns about:\n   - ceo-boot.py:120 redefines check_tier_a_spec_version_drift after TIER_A_CHECKS (scheduled_cache.py:376) has already bound the copy at model_persona.py:6, so the facade copy is dead for the registry.\n   - budget-summary.py:400 repeats _load_normalize_model_name from cost_rollup.py.\n   - native_reconciliation.py:3-6 repeats the MEMORY_CLAIM_* constants that set the validate_memory_claim defaults at :566-569; editing budget-summary.py:102-105 will not change those defaults.\n   Fix: keep a single definition of each.\n6. Each fragment's leading docstring is stored as the facade's __doc__ (check_bash_safety.__doc__ becomes the decisions_cli.py docstring; ceo-boot likewise). No consumer found; cosmetic.\n7. normalization.py:4-55 carries a stale second copy of the old module docstring (a mid-file #! line, steps numbered 1,2,4,5). Remove it.\n8. The import at posture_scan.py:571 sits outside any try. A partial upgrade without bash_indirect_guard.py gives allow plus a FATAL line on every command that passes the legacy checks: the direction is correct, but main() cannot tell an infra fault from a scanner bug. Separate the two as part of the P1 fix.","sentence":"No declared condition is false against this payload and no P0 was found, but the new indirect-execution guard has an undeclared P1: about 1000 nested xargs drive its recursion into a RecursionError, which main() turns into an allow, contradicting the CHANGELOG's fail-closed-at-depth claim, so the release should carry that P1 as an open annex item."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4132,"cache_read_input_tokens":217246,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4132},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd5ebd56-59b6-4268-812f-e9994cacc38a","uuid":"83d1782d-a8a4-4dda-adb7-ff45eebfaa7b","timestamp":"2026-11-15T09:33:18.156Z","request_id":"req_011Ch4Uw77an19mTb8WAoGdu","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GoYfnKRag7D2CYn2cPhrHJ":{"decision":"GO-WITH-CONDITIONS","report":"SCOPE: payload 5/32 = new fragment files .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/{normalization,posture_parser,posture_scan,posture_tables,rewrite_citation}.py, .claude/hooks/_lib/_budget_summary_parts/*.py, .claude/hooks/_lib/_ceo_boot_parts/__init__.py, judged together with their facades (check_bash_safety.py, .claude/scripts/budget-summary.py, .claude/scripts/ceo-boot.py) and the PLAN-182 staged pre-split monolith .claude/plans/PLAN-182/staged-w1/tree/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py as the ordering reference.\n\nVERIFIED (no finding):\n- Split fidelity: check_bash_safety.py:20-39 execs the 8 parts in order into the facade globals; the parts are contiguous and in monolith order (normalization ends at _FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE_DISABLE_VAR and rewrite_citation starts at _FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE_CLASS; rewrite_citation ends at _extract_transcript_path and fact_state starts at _apply_destructive_citation_gate; fact_canonical ends at _E3_BODY_CAP_BYTES and posture_tables starts at _e3_check_canonical_path_write; posture_parser starts at _E4_PREFIX_RUNNER_FLAGS; posture_scan starts at _E4_EXPANSION_RE; decisions_cli starts at _emit_env_var_hijack_event). Module-level dependencies are defined first (_e3_basename at fact_canonical.py:311 precedes the posture_tables use).\n- Namespace semantics: the monolith already used `from __future__ import annotations` (staged copy line 60) and compile() inherits it, so annotation semantics are unchanged. __file__ is the facade path, so _HOOKS_DIR = .claude/hooks as before. The `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` guard in decisions_cli.py:252 fires only as a script. Test patch points on the facade reach every part because the namespace is shared.\n- budget-summary rebinding (budget-summary.py:371-397) rebinds every part function to the facade globals. Every global the parts use is defined in the facade. Part defaults reference only names local to the part. _lib is a regular package, so the __init__-less _budget_summary_parts imports as a namespace subpackage on Python 3.9.\n- Installer: scripts/install.sh:1520-1547 cp -R's _lib subdirectories, so the three fragment directories ship. The canonical guard `.claude/hooks/_lib/**/*.py` (canonical_edit/guards.py:34) covers them. check_arbitration_kernel.py:314 lists _bash_safety_parts (condition 5).\n- Condition 6 holds: posture_scan.py:572-580 reads CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD / CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS from the trusted_env snapshot (CEO_ prefix, trusted_env.py:36). Only \"0\" disables. A missing snapshot leaves the guard ON.\n- Fail direction: a missing or broken fragment or module, or an import error, makes the hook crash with no decision, and the shim lets the command through (fail-open on infra, doctrine). The fact gate stays fail-open on infra and closed on citation verification (moved code, unchanged).\n- Item 6: stdlib only; no 3.10+ syntax in the three fragment directories.\n- Conditions 5 and 7 are consistent with this payload; no declared condition is contradicted.\n\nP0: none.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\nP1 — .claude/hooks/_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py:386-393 (xargs; also :394-399 find -exec), with call site .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_scan.py:571-584 and catch-all .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/decisions_cli.py:243-249.\nFailure scenario: _scan_segment (bash_indirect_guard.py:347) recurses once per xargs level with no depth check; MAX_DEPTH is enforced only in _scan (:472). A command `xargs xargs ...(about 1000 times) rm -rf ~` stays under MAX_WORDS=1024 and MAX_BODY_BYTES=16384. The legacy matchers never see `rm` (tokens[0] is xargs; _e3 and _e4 walk iteratively without hitting it). The new scan then raises RecursionError at the default limit of 1000. decide_command does not catch it, and main()'s `except Exception` emits allow with a FATAL line on stderr, so GNU xargs runs rm -rf ~. This is fail-OPEN on input a security matcher cannot handle (item 2). The repo's own doctrine calls this out: posture_parser.py:369 says a RecursionError in a security matcher is a crash, i.e. a fail-open. It also contradicts CHANGELOG.md:93-94 (\"fails closed ... at its depth, size and word limits\"). It is not a regression vs the base, which never blocked xargs-wrapped rm -rf at all, and it needs a deliberately built ~1000-token command, so it is not P0.\nMinimal fix: add `if depth > MAX_DEPTH: return _hit(\"bash_indirect_limit\")` at the top of _scan_segment. Optionally, at posture_scan.py:578, map a RecursionError from scan() to a bash_indirect_limit block while keeping ImportError fail-open. Add a regression test with ~1000 nested xargs.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. check_bash_safety.py:12 (facade docstring) says _apply_destructive_citation_gate is implemented in rewrite_citation.py; it is at fact_state.py:4. A maintainer re-applying a hand patch (condition 7) is sent to the wrong file. Fix: correct the marker.\n2. rewrite_citation.py:274 still defines DESTRUCTIVE as rm -rf / git reset --hard / git push --force. posture_scan.py:582 now marks every bash_indirect_* hit destructive=True, including bash_indirect_limit, bash_indirect_unparseable_shell and bash_indirect_opaque_stdin, so they become releasable through the opt-in fact gate (enforce) or the citation pilot. Fix: update the comment, or exclude the limit/unparseable codes from release.\n3. bash_indirect_guard.py:472 blocks any command longer than 16384 characters or 1024 words, even a direct one (a large heredoc written through Bash, `git add` with more than 1000 paths). The block reason tells the user to \"write the executable command in direct form\", which misleads for a command that is already direct. CHANGELOG.md:465-470 (adopter notes) omits the caps; :93-94 mentions them only generically. This is within condition 6's declared unmeasured-false-positive caveat. Fix: a distinct reason text for the limit block, and document the numbers.\n4. When the fact gate in enforce mode (fact_canonical.py:146-155) or the citation pilot (fact_state.py:23-65) reshapes an indirect block, the new Decision drops reason_code, so emit_veto_triggered(reason_code=bash_indirect_*) at decisions_cli.py:195 is skipped in those opt-in modes. Fix: carry reason_code through.\n5. Duplicates left by the split, a drift trap that condition 7 itself warns about:\n   - ceo-boot.py:120 redefines check_tier_a_spec_version_drift after TIER_A_CHECKS (scheduled_cache.py:376) has already bound the copy at model_persona.py:6, so the facade copy is dead for the registry.\n   - budget-summary.py:400 repeats _load_normalize_model_name from cost_rollup.py.\n   - native_reconciliation.py:3-6 repeats the MEMORY_CLAIM_* constants that set the validate_memory_claim defaults at :566-569; editing budget-summary.py:102-105 will not change those defaults.\n   Fix: keep a single definition of each.\n6. Each fragment's leading docstring is stored as the facade's __doc__ (check_bash_safety.__doc__ becomes the decisions_cli.py docstring; ceo-boot likewise). No consumer found; cosmetic.\n7. normalization.py:4-55 carries a stale second copy of the old module docstring (a mid-file #! line, steps numbered 1,2,4,5). Remove it.\n8. The import at posture_scan.py:571 sits outside any try. A partial upgrade without bash_indirect_guard.py gives allow plus a FATAL line on every command that passes the legacy checks: the direction is correct, but main() cannot tell an infra fault from a scanner bug. Separate the two as part of the P1 fix.","sentence":"No declared condition is false against this payload and no P0 was found, but the new indirect-execution guard has an undeclared P1: about 1000 nested xargs drive its recursion into a RecursionError, which main() turns into an allow, contradicting the CHANGELOG's fail-closed-at-depth claim, so the release should carry that P1 as an open annex item."}}}
