t P0.\nMinimal fix: add `if depth > MAX_DEPTH: return _hit(\"bash_indirect_limit\")` at the top of _scan_segment. Optionally, at posture_scan.py:578, map a RecursionError from scan() to a bash_indirect_limit block while keeping ImportError fail-open. Add a regression test with ~1000 nested xargs.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. check_bash_safety.py:12 (facade docstring) says _apply_destructive_citation_gate is implemented in rewrite_citation.py; it is at fact_state.py:4. A maintainer re-applying a hand patch (condition 7) is sent to the wrong file. Fix: correct the marker.\n2. rewrite_citation.py:274 still defines DESTRUCTIVE as rm -rf / git reset --hard / git push --force. posture_scan.py:582 now marks every bash_indirect_* hit destructive=True, including bash_indirect_limit, bash_indirect_unparseable_shell and bash_indirect_opaque_stdin, so they become releasable through the opt-in fact gate (enforce) or the citation pilot. Fix: update the comment, or exclude the limit/unparseable codes from release.\n3. bash_indirect_guard.py:472 blocks any command longer than 16384 characters or 1024 words, even a direct one (a large heredoc written through Bash, `git add` with more than 1000 paths). The block reason tells the user to \"write the executable command in direct form\", which misleads for a command that is already direct. CHANGELOG.md:465-470 (adopter notes) omits the caps; :93-94 mentions them only generically. This is within condition 6's declared unmeasured-false-positive caveat. Fix: a distinct reason text for the limit block, and document the numbers.\n4. When the fact gate in enforce mode (fact_canonical.py:146-155) or the citation pilot (fact_state.py:23-65) reshapes an indirect block, the new Decision drops reason_code, so emit_veto_triggered(reason_code=bash_indirect_*) at decisions_cli.py:195 is skipped in those opt-in modes. Fix: carry reason_code through.\n5. Duplicates left by the split, a drift trap that condition 7 itself warns about:\n   - ceo-boot.py:120 redefines check_tier_a_spec_version_drift after TIER_A_CHECKS (scheduled_cache.py:376) has already bound the copy at model_persona.py:6, so the facade copy is dead for the registry.\n   - budget-summary.py:400 repeats _load_normalize_model_name from cost_rollup.py.\n   - native_reconciliation.py:3-6 repeats the MEMORY_CLAIM_* constants that set the validate_memory_claim defaults at :566-569; editing budget-summary.py:102-105 will not change those defaults.\n   Fix: keep a single definition of each.\n6. Each fragment's leading docstring is stored as the facade's __doc__ (check_bash_safety.__doc__ becomes the decisions_cli.py docstring; ceo-boot likewise). No consumer found; cosmetic.\n7. normalization.py:4-55 carries a stale second copy of the old module docstring (a mid-file #! line, steps numbered 1,2,4,5). Remove it.\n8. The import at posture_scan.py:571 sits outside any try. A partial upgrade without bash_indirect_guard.py gives allow plus a FATAL line on every command that passes the legacy checks: the direction is correct, but main() cannot tell an infra fault from a scanner bug. Separate the two as part of the P1 fix.","sentence":"No declared condition is false against this payload and no P0 was found, but the new indirect-execution guard has an undeclared P1: about 1000 nested xargs drive its recursion into a RecursionError, which main() turns into an allow, contradicting the CHANGELOG's fail-closed-at-depth claim, so the release should carry that P1 as an open annex item."},"ttft_ms":20882,"type":"result","duration_ms":553204,"uuid":"b2049d18-bae3-483c-bc49-75700862b786","ttft_stream_ms":5729,"time_to_request_ms":184,"first_content_frame_ms":5729,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":78,"outputTokens":48833,"cacheReadInputTokens":6347268,"cacheCreationInputTokens":216905,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":3.981665599999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":34479,"cache